From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: knfsd-fuzz <knfsd-fuzz@localhost>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kcov: keep coverage callbacks out of sibling calls

GCC turns a coverage callback at the end of an instrumented function into a
tail jump to __sanitizer_cov_trace_pc(). The callback records _RET_IP_, and
after a jump that is the return address in the instrumented function's
caller, not a site in the instrumented function. A GCC 11.4 amd64 KASAN
build had 14,239 such jumps, and a remote KCOV run published 323 PCs that
were ordinary call sites in other functions, while the instrumented paths
behind those jumps were never reported.

Disable sibling-call optimization for KCOV-instrumented objects only. In
the same build no tail jump to the callback remained, and every published
PC was a callback call site.
---
 scripts/Makefile.kcov | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/scripts/Makefile.kcov b/scripts/Makefile.kcov
index 78305a8..27a9624 100644
--- a/scripts/Makefile.kcov
+++ b/scripts/Makefile.kcov
@@ -1,6 +1,9 @@
 # SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
 kcov-flags-y					+= -fsanitize-coverage=trace-pc
 kcov-flags-$(CONFIG_KCOV_ENABLE_COMPARISONS)	+= -fsanitize-coverage=trace-cmp
+# A tail jump to __sanitizer_cov_trace_pc() makes _RET_IP_ the caller's return
+# address instead of the instrumented call site.
+kcov-flags-y					+= -fno-optimize-sibling-calls
 
 kcov-rflags-y					+= -Cpasses=sancov-module
 kcov-rflags-y					+= -Cllvm-args=-sanitizer-coverage-level=3
-- 
